WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.260
Narrator: Does this scream sound familiar?

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.053
That's because it's
used in over 100 films.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.350
It's a movie trope called
the Wilhelm Scream.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.100
Jonathan Kuntz: The
Wilhelm Scream is maybe

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.550
the most famous sound effect in Hollywood,

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.822
that is used when characters are shot

00:00:27.822 --> 00:00:32.050
or fall off high places in action films.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.600
Narrator: The scream
made its first appearance

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.800
in a 1951 film called "Distant Drums."

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:37.950
Kuntz: And there's a moment in the film

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.320
where a character is
dragged into the swamp,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.737
and they wanted a particularly
blood-curdling scream.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:46.580
Narrator: Now, this was just one

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.590
of many different screams in the film.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.020
Kuntz: To make a film, you
need a sound effects library,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.520
a collection of sound effects.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.710
And you're going to use them
in different parts of the film.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.850
Maybe in one part of your action film,

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.810
you need a two-second scream,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:03.870
but in another part of your film,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.137
you might need a one-and-a-half-second

00:01:05.137 --> 00:01:06.950
blood-curdling scream.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.580
Narrator: Producers
had cast members record

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.840
a variety of different screams.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.010
But there was one man's
scream in particular

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.620
that stood out among the rest.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.890
Kuntz: It's usually
credited now to Sheb Wooley,

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.210
who was a kind of a western
style character actor

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.430
and a novelty singer

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.220
who appears in "Distant Drums."

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.588
Narrator: But its first appearance in 1951

00:01:26.588 --> 00:01:29.130
wasn't the scream's shining moment.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.367
That happened when it was used again

00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:35.350
in the 1953 film "The
Charge at Feather River."

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.350
A character named Private
Wilhelm was shot in the leg

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:39.517
with an arrow.

00:01:41.438 --> 00:01:44.611
Kuntz: And that's how it got
named the Wilhelm Scream.

00:01:44.611 --> 00:01:45.871
Man: Wilhelm!

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:48.350
Wilhelm: Yeah, I'll just fill my pipe.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.580
Narrator: Since then,
the sound has been used

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.693
in more than 100 films.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.110
Why?

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.806
Well, for one, it's
cheaper and easier to reuse

00:02:03.806 --> 00:02:05.730
preexisting sound effects.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.140
Kuntz: You have to give
credit to Ben Burtt,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.100
the wonderful sound designer,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.361
who's been working on the
biggest films in Hollywood

00:02:11.361 --> 00:02:14.950
since the 1970s, a close
associate of George Lucas.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.060
He is the one who really
discovered the Wilhelm Scream

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.170
and researched out its origins.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.350
Narrator: Burtt used the
scream in a memorable moment

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.550
in "Star Wars."

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.937
Kuntz: When Luke Skywalker
fires at the Stormtrooper.

00:02:27.937 --> 00:02:29.472
Narrator: The scream
actually appears in all

00:02:29.472 --> 00:02:31.581
of the original "Star Wars" films.

00:02:31.581 --> 00:02:34.430
Kuntz: And then it became an in-joke.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.530
Narrator: The scream then spread

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:36.363
to many other famous films.

00:02:37.621 --> 00:02:39.237
Kuntz: Steven Spielberg picked up on it

00:02:39.237 --> 00:02:41.288
and used it in many, many of his films.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.040
And then it just went crazy, of course.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.715
Peter Jackson uses it in
"The Lord of the Rings."

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.877
It even shows up in "Toy
Story" and "Reservoir Dogs."

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.040
Narrator: The sound is now
famous among film buffs.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.570
Kuntz: I think you can go on YouTube

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.790
and see compilations of
dozens of Wilhelm screams.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.520
I mean, it has its own Wikipedia entry.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.670
And so, for people that love movies,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.410
this is part of their enjoyment of it,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.577
is finding this little insider detail.

